                "type": "paragraph",
                "text": "Consider two investors.<div><span style=\"font-size: 1.1rem;\"><font color=\"#ff8c00\"><b>Investor A</b></font></span></div><div><ul><li>Invests ₹10 lakh in one transaction.</li><li>Holds it for five years.</li></ul></div><div><b><font color=\"#ff8c00\">Investor B</font></b></div><div><ul><li>Invests ₹20,000 every month for five years.</li><li>Makes additional investments during market corrections.</li><li>Redeems some money after three years.</li></ul></div><div>At the end of five years, both portfolios are worth the same amount.</div><div><br></div><div>Does that mean they earned identical returns?</div><div><br></div><div>Not necessarily.</div><div><br></div><div>Investor B's cash flows occurred on multiple dates.</div><div>Some investments had five years to grow.</div><div>Others had only a few months.</div><div><br></div><div>Only XIRR can accurately calculate the return generated by such investment patterns.</div><div><br></div><div>This is why professional portfolio managers, mutual fund houses, and wealth advisors rely on XIRR instead of simple return calculations.</div>"
